\description{
Cuts the score into bands of equal share (or into tail percentiles)
\strong{frozen on the reference sample}, and reads every band on the reference
and on the study samples: volume, event rate with a Jeffreys interval,
lift, capture (recall), the cumulative non-event share (false positive
rate), KS, the band WOE and IV, odds, the PSI term against the reference
and a one-sided Fisher exact test of rank order against the previous band.
The summary adds the AUC, Gini and KS of every sample with a bootstrap
interval.
}
\section{Method}{


The scored rows are aggregated once into a table of counts per distinct
score (per sample); every statistic is then computed from that table, so
the cost is one pass over the rows plus work proportional to the number
of distinct scores. With more than \code{max_cells} distinct scores, the
scores are first pooled into \code{max_cells} cells of equal weighted share.

The cut targets are cumulative shares counted from the event-rich side
of the score (the high scores under \code{higher_is_riskier}, the low ones
under \code{higher_is_safer}): \code{k / n_bands} with \code{spacing = "uniform"}, or
the shares in \code{tail_probs} with \code{spacing = "tail"} (default 0.1\%, 0.5\%,
1\%, 2\%, 5\%, 10\%, 20\% and 50\%). Each cut is placed midway between two
adjacent distinct reference scores, at the boundary nearest to its
target, so a group of tied scores is never split and a target is hit
within the share of one score value. Targets that land on the same
boundary give one cut: \code{n_bands_effective} can be smaller than
\code{n_bands_requested}, and both are reported. A band is left-closed,
\verb{[lo, hi)}: \code{score >= cut} is the upper side, the convention of
\code{\link[=scr_cutoff]{scr_cutoff()}}. \code{breaks} given explicitly are used as they are; the bands
of \code{\link[=scr_score_gains]{scr_score_gains()}} (\code{breaks = sc$breaks}) are right-closed, so a
score equal to a break falls one band higher here.

The band table lists the event-richest band first (\code{band = 1}). With
\eqn{e_b} events and \eqn{m_b} non-events in band \eqn{b}, totals \eqn{E}
and \eqn{M}, and overall rate \eqn{R}:
\itemize{
\item \code{rate} = \eqn{e_b / (e_b + m_b)}, with the Jeffreys interval
\code{rate_lo}, \code{rate_hi}: the Beta(\eqn{e_b + 1/2, m_b + 1/2}) quantiles,
0 and 1 at the edges (Brown, Cai and DasGupta, 2001). Under weights,
the counts are scaled to the Kish effective size
\eqn{(\sum w)^2 / \sum w^2}.
\item \code{lift} = \code{rate} / \eqn{R} (its interval divides the rate bounds by
\eqn{R}); \code{cum_rate} and \code{cum_lift} accumulate from the first band.
\item \code{capture} = \eqn{\sum_{j \le b} e_j / E} (recall),
\code{cum_nonevent_pct} = \eqn{\sum_{j \le b} m_j / M} (false positive
rate) and \code{ks} their absolute difference.
\item \code{pct_event}, \code{pct_nonevent} and \code{woe} = \eqn{\ln(e_b / E) -
    \ln(m_b / M)} as in \code{\link[=scr_strategy]{scr_strategy()}} (0.5 is added to every band only
when a band lacks events or non-events); \code{iv} =
(\code{pct_event} - \code{pct_nonevent}) * \code{woe}.
\item \code{odds} and \code{log_odds} in the orientation of the scale
(non-events per event under \code{higher_is_safer}, events per non-event
under \code{higher_is_riskier}, 0.5 added to each count), as in
\code{\link[=scr_score_gains]{scr_score_gains()}}.
\item \code{psi}: the band term of the PSI against the reference shares
(see \code{\link[=scr_psi]{scr_psi()}}); \code{NA} on the reference itself.
\item \code{p_reversal}: one-sided Fisher exact test that the band has a
\strong{higher} event rate than the previous, event-richer band (a
reversal of the rank order); \code{p_reversal_adj} is Holm-adjusted over
the bands of the sample. The tests use the unweighted counts.
}

Rows with a missing or infinite score, or a zero weight, are not counted;
rows with a missing outcome count in the volume (\code{n}, \code{pct}, the PSI) but
not in the rates.

The bootstrap of the AUC draws the event and non-event counts of every
score value from multinomial laws with the observed shares (the law of a
row bootstrap stratified by outcome), with the unweighted class counts as
sizes; a given \code{seed} is local to the call, while \code{seed = NULL} draws
from, and advances, the user's random stream. Up to \code{boot_cells} cells of
the count table the bootstrap is exact. Above it, the resamples run on
\code{boot_cells} cells of equal share (adjacent cells pooled) and are shifted
to the point estimate of the full table, so the interval is an
approximation. The point estimates use every cell of the count table:
every distinct score, unless \code{max_cells} pooled the scores into cells.
\code{boot_cells = Inf} keeps the bootstrap exact, at a cost per resample
proportional to the number of cells.
}

